====Adjective====
{{en-adj|er}}

# {{sid|en|physically elevated}} Physically [[elevated]], extending above a base or average level:
## {{sid|en|far above base}} Very [[elevated]]; extending or being far above a [[base]]; [[tall]]; [[lofty]].
##: {{ux|en|The balloon rose '''high''' in the sky. &nbsp; The wall was '''high'''. &nbsp; a '''high''' mountain}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1612|author=w:Michael Drayton|title=w:Poly-Olbion|section=song 13 p. 214|sectionurl=http://poly-olbion.exeter.ac.uk/the-text/full-text/song-13/
|passage=Upon the '''highest''' spray of every mounting pole,<br>Those Quirristers are pearcht with many a speckled breast.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1930|author=Philip Sidney Smith|title=Mineral Industry of Alaska in 1928 and Administration Report|passage=The Chitistone River Valley offers a more direct route for travel from McCarthy to the White River and the Shushana gold placers than Skolai Creek, but it involves a '''high''' climb over the so-called “goat trail” to avoid the canyon above Chitistone[.]}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|date=2013-06-07| author=David Simpson| volume=188| issue=26| page=36| magazine=w:The Guardian Weekly| url=http://www.guardian.co.uk/books/2013/may/18/falling-upwards-richard-holmes-review|title=Fantasy of navigation| passage=Like most human activities, ballooning has sponsored heroes and hucksters and a good deal in between. For every dedicated scientist patiently recording atmospheric pressure and wind speed while shivering at '''high''' altitudes, there is a carnival barker with a bevy of pretty girls willing to dangle from a basket or parachute down to earth.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20250426235601/https://www.theguardian.com/books/2013/may/18/falling-upwards-richard-holmes-review}}
## {{sid|en|above normal elevation}} Relatively elevated; rising or raised above the average or normal level from which elevation is measured.
##* {{RQ:Travers Cuckoo in the Nest|chapter=1|passage=She was like a Beardsley ''Salome'', he had said. And indeed she had the narrow eyes and the '''high''' cheekbone of that creature, and as nearly the sinuosity as is compatible with human symmetry. His wooing had been brief but incisive.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1919|author=Martha Van Rensselaer; Flora Rose; Helen Canon|title=A Manual of Home-Making|page=376|passage=A nightgown with a '''high''' neck and long sleeves may have the fullness set into a yoke.}}
##* {{RQ:Guardian|title=How our home delivery habit reshaped the world|author=Samanth Subramanian|date=November 21 2019|url=https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2019/nov/21/how-our-home-delivery-habit-reshaped-the-world|passage=The concrete floors of B2B sheds were already being built to an exacting degree of flatness, calibrated using lasers, so that forklifts would not teeter while lifting pallets to the '''highest''' shelves.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20250124100530/https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2019/nov/21/how-our-home-delivery-habit-reshaped-the-world}}
## {{sid|en|baseball}} {{lb|en|baseball}} Of a [[ball]], above the batter's shoulders.
##: {{ux|en|the pitch (or: the ball) was '''high'''}}
## {{sid|en|mountainous}} Pertaining to (''or, especially of a language'': spoken in) an area which is at a greater elevation, for example more [[mountainous]], than other regions.
# {{sid|en|of specified elevation}} Having a specified elevation or height; [[tall]].
#: {{ux|en|three feet '''high''''' &nbsp; ''three Mount Everests '''high'''}}
#* {{RQ:Lincoln Pratt's Patients|chapter=4|passage=I told him about everything I could think of; and what I couldn't think of he did. He asked about six questions during my yarn, but every question had a point to it. At the end he bowed and thanked me once more. As a thanker he was main-truck '''high'''; I never see anybody so polite.}}
# {{sid|en|status}} {{cap|elevated}} in [[status]], [[esteem]], or [[prestige]], or in importance or development; [[exalted]] in rank, station, or character.
#: {{ux|en|The oldest of the elves' royal family still conversed in '''High''' Elvish.}}
#* {{RQ:Dickens Little Dorrit|passage=The Barnacles were a very '''high''' family, and a very large family. They were dispersed all over the public offices, and held all sorts of public places.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2007|author=w:Sheila Finch|title=Shaper's Legacy|isbn=1434401596|page=122
|passage=Not a one of them was old enough to know what the '''high''' past of Liani separatism had really been like.}}
## {{sid|en|most exalted}} Most exalted; [[foremost]].
##: {{ux|en|the '''high''' priest'', ''the '''high''' officials of the court'', ''the '''high''' altar}}
## {{sid|en|great importance}} Of great importance and [[consequence]]: [[grave]] (if negative) or [[solemn]] (if positive).
##: {{ux|en|'''high''' crimes'', ''the '''high''' festival of the sun}}
## {{sid|en|advanced in development}} {{cap|consummate}}; advanced (e.g. in development) to the utmost extent or culmination, or possessing a quality in its supreme degree, at its [[zenith]].
##: {{ux|en|'''high''' (i.e. intense) heat; '''high''' (i.e. full or quite) noon; '''high''' (i.e. rich or spicy) seasoning; '''high''' (i.e. complete) pleasure; '''high''' (i.e. deep or vivid) colour; '''high''' (i.e. extensive, thorough) scholarship; '''high''' tide; '''high''' [tourism] season; the '''High''' Middle Ages}}
##* {{RQ:Spenser Amoretti|passage='''High''' time it is this war now ended were.}}
##* '''1709-1710''', {{w|Thomas Baker (antiquarian)|Thomas Baker}}, ''Reflections on Learning''
##*: '''High''' sauces and rich spices are fetch'd from the Indies.
## {{sid|en|advanced in complexity}} {{cap|advanced}} in complexity (and hence potentially abstract and/or difficult to comprehend).
##* {{RQ:Shakespeare Julius Caesar|I|ii|passage=to hear and answer such '''high''' things}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1802|author=w:William Wordsworth|title=England 1802
|passage=Plain living and '''high''' thinking are no more.}}
# {{sid|en|traditionalist}} {{lb|en|of an opinion or practice|obsolete|_|outside set phrases}} [[extreme|Extreme]], [[excessive]]; {{ng|now specifically}} very [[traditionalist]] and [[conservative]].
#: {{ux|en|[[high church|'''high''' church]] &nbsp; [[High Tory|'''High''' Tory]]}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1591|author=w:Henry Barrow|title=A Plaine Refutation of M. G. Giffardes reprochful booke{{nb...}}|url=https://www.proquest.com/eebo/docview/2240894050/99840237|page=33|text=Furder, what wil you answeare for your keping a daye, cessation &c to'' St. Michael & al Angells''? how wil you excuse your self of most '''high''' idolatrie, advancing your self in thinges you neuer sawe, rashlie puffed vp of your fleshlie minde, and not holding the heade, depriuing others of their Crowne?}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1858|author=Joseph Howe|title=Speeches and Public Letters|page=346
|passage=The letter of a "Pioneer" was sent to the Chronicle office by a very respectable man, of a '''high''' conservative family, but whose interests have been injuriously affected by the constant fluctuations in the commercial policy of England.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1875|author={{w|Henry Reeve (journalist)|Henry Reeve}}|chapter=Introductory Notice|mainauthor=w:Alexis de Tocqueville|translator=Henry Reeve|title=w:Democracy in America|page=xvi|pageurl=https://books.google.co.uk/books?id=BGgFAAAAQAAJ&pg=PR16|text=His family was ardently royalist, and might be compared to a '''high''' Tory family on this side the water; with some change of conditions, their prejudices and disposition of the mind were the same.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2005|author=Jesse D. Geller; John C. Norcross; David E. Orlinsky|title=The Psychotherapist's Own Psychotherapy|publisher=Oxford University Press|isbn=9780198030621|page=69
|passage=My father was the youngest son of a '''High'''-Church and '''high''' Tory family, the politically leftwing and religiously Nonconformist rebel; and antiimperialist who nearly lost his position in the City by refusing to sign his firm's pro–Boer War petition.}}
