#* {{quote-book|en|year=1966|title=Symposium on Antarctic Oceanography: Papers|page=242
|passage=But other euphausiids, ''Euphausia crystallorophias'', are found in the pack ice region of the '''high''' Antarctic as food of Blue and Minke Whales (Marr, 1956). ''E. vallentini'' is very important in the lower Antarctic region, around {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1990|author=International Union of Game Biologists|publisher=Congress|title=Transactions, the XIXth IUGB Congress: Population dynamics|page=219
|passage=We predict that ''L. arctica'' will coincide with the whole reindeer-caribou distribution, probably excepted Svalbard, South Georgia and other '''high'''-polar areas.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1999|author=Peter John Hodum|title=Foraging Ecology and Reproductive Energetics of Antarctic Fulmarine Petrels|page=8
|passage={{...}} petrels, which breed primarily in the '''high''' Antarctic, the Rauer Islands are fairly central in their breeding distribution. This study documents the breeding biology of these four species of fulmarine petrels on Hop Island, Rauer Islands during&nbsp;{{...}}}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2004|title=Berichte zur Polar- und Meeresforschung|volume=481-483|page=1
|passage=Except for some lithodid crabs that have recently been found in the Antarctic Bellingshausen Sea (Klages et al., 1995; Arana and Retamal, 2000), reptants are not known from '''high''' polar areas, where water temperature at the seafloor drops permanently below about 0&deg;C.}}
#* '''2007''', ''Zoological Studies'', volume 46, iissues 1-3, page 371:
#*: This study also analyzed the sources of variations over an environmental gradient extending from low (subtropical) to '''high''' (sub-Antarctic) latitudes.
# {{sid|en|large quantity}} {{cap|large}}, [[great]] (in amount or quantity, value, force, energy, etc).
